na = (i:1) (t:pron) (f:everyday) (w:you/your) (d:second person marker: before a verb it means you, before a noun your) (s:nang) (r:2.20.3)
na = (i:3) (t:part) (f:everyday) (w:already/beforehand) (d:placed before a verb whose subject is not you, it shows that the action took place earlier or away from the speaker, as in {na om hi}, was there; often left untranslated, while the pronoun {na} means you or your) (r:ts:3271/ts:301/ts:3508) (q:draft, low confidence)
na = (i:4) (t:v) (f:health/body) (w:be ill/hurt/be in pain) (d:be sick or in pain, said of the body or a part of it, as in {a ci hong na}, he or she fell ill; before a following word the form is <nat>) (r:ts:3369) (q:draft, low confidence)
